mplement the different microfrontend containers (\\\"apps\\\"). One of these hacks is a library called `import-map-overrides`, which is _primarily_ intended as a developer tool, but which also included some nice functionality not available in default browser implementations of import maps include:\\r\\n\\r\\n* Support for multiple import maps on a single page\\r\\n* Support for loading import maps via `<script>` tags with a `src` attribute (which is how we currently do things)\\r\\n\\r\\nIt also provides tooling that is useful solely in dev-mode, like providing import map entries via `localStorage` and leveraging those to override dependencies defined in the \\\"static\\\" import map included in the page. Unfortunately, supporting the things we want enabled at runtime even in production also meant supporting the things we didn't want running in production at runtime. Additionally, our `routes.registry.json` handling (the main file that describes all of the entry points that a microfrontend provides to the framework) is built on the model of `import-map-overrides` as so has the exact same issues.\\r\\n\\r\\nEssentially, because of the way things were working, and import map overrides or route overrides that are stored in `localStorage` would be incorporated into the effective import map or effective route registry, even in production mode, which means that these could be used by someone to unexpectedly load a remote definition of a microfrontend (preventing this is one of the reasons that the RefApp runs with a reasonably strict Content Security Policy). This type of tooling is great in dev mode when we actually do want to load scripts from remote locations (locally running microfrontends).\\r\\n\\r\\nIn order to keep the functions that we want in production mode while only allowing the `localStorage` overrides in development mode, it was necessary to remove the `import-map-overrides` and replace it with our own shim that maintains the same API but where we can selectively disable parts of it.\\r\\n\\r\\nWe further have a feature, `spaEnv`, which has been part of the framework for a while without actively being used for anything which allows implementations to declare whether an instance is intended for \\\"development\\\", \\\"test\\\", or \\\"production\\\". In \\\"production\\\" mode (the default), the framework will not load any import map overrides or routes overrides. This also means that this PR deprecates the ability to access the import map overrides via `localStorage`.\\r\\n\\r\\nFor the most part, I don't anticipate these changes having any impact on most day-to-day workflows. The one workflow it affects is the one where you go to a running server like dev3, type `localStorage.setItem(\\\"openmrs:devtools\\\", true)`, refresh the page and get access to the devtools. To work with the devtools you will either need:\\r\\n\\r\\n1. A server running in development mode\\r\\n2. A local server\\r\\n\\r\\nWorkflows using `yarn start` or `yarn run:shell` are unaffected. If you need something closer to what's been disabled, you should be able to run the CLI's `start` command (`yarn openmrs start`), which just runs a local copy of the app shell, also in development mode.\\r\\n\\r\\n## Screenshots\\r\\n<!-- Required if you are making UI changes. -->\\r\\n\\r\\n## Related Issue\\r\\n<!-- Paste the link to the Jira ticket here if one exists. -->\\r\\n<!-- https://issues.openmrs.org/browse/O3- -->\\r\\n\\r\\n## Other\\r\\n<!-- Anything not covered above -->\\r\\n\\r\\nIn addition to the above this PR also \\\"freezes\\\" the properties that the framework sets to their values at the point the framework is initialized. Since this is client-side JS, this obviously isn't perfect, but it should make those properties behave closer to the read-only constants we treat them as.\\r\\n\\r\\nEventually, we'll probably replace this stuff with something like [es-module-shims](https://github.com/guybedford/es-module-shims), but that's deferred until everything actually becomes real ES modules (some day!)\\r\\n\",\"freshness\":[{\"repo\":\"github.com/openmrs/openmrs-esm-core\",\"lastSyncedAt\":\"2026-10-31T16:11:06.195Z\",\"stale\":false}],\"nextOffset\":null,\"truncated\":[\"files: showing 61 of 66\"]}"}],"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01SFeZEoYPX9yCtkoycHgq7q","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
